1
00:00:26,042 --> 00:00:41,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the other day we were talking about anicca, and then you started talking about it in terms of uncertainty, and you used that term, what if.

2
00:00:44,803 --> 00:00:48,985
[SPEAKER_00]: However, as we were speaking, you said, you came...

3
00:00:50,665 --> 00:00:53,486
[SPEAKER_00]: I said to you, that's why nibbāna is undetermined.

4
00:00:53,506 --> 00:00:54,887
[SPEAKER_00]: We were speaking about nibbāna.

5
00:00:55,047 --> 00:00:56,487
[SPEAKER_00]: I said, nibbāna is undetermined.

6
00:00:57,288 --> 00:01:00,649
[SPEAKER_00]: And you said, exactly, uncertainty is the undetermined.

7
00:01:01,969 --> 00:01:03,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was...

8
00:01:03,550 --> 00:01:04,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I thought, huh?

9
00:01:04,971 --> 00:01:06,591
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not what I meant, actually.

10
00:01:10,747 --> 00:01:22,536
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if we talk about that again, the undetermined, you said, like, uncertainty is the undetermined when when it's purified of ignorance.

11
00:01:22,856 --> 00:01:33,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, just, just, you start, you start on a basic level, just colloquially speaking, if something is determined, it implies certainty.

12
00:01:34,906 --> 00:01:36,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertain things are less determined.

13
00:01:37,648 --> 00:01:40,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's not so determined, so it's uncertain.

14
00:01:40,598 --> 00:01:56,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the absolute pinnacle of the undetermined thing is nibbāna dhamma, the phenomenon of nibbāna, the phenomenon of complete asankhata dhamma, as the suttas would say.

15
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:16,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what... from the point of view of a mind that depends on certainty, that depends on nicha and permanence, and taking things personally, basically, putujana's mind, an ignorant mind, nibbāna is the ultimate uncertainty, the most frightening thing, basically.

16
00:02:17,766 --> 00:02:24,508
[SPEAKER_01]: But uncertainty is frightening only if you still take certainty for granted and put it first.

17
00:02:25,486 --> 00:02:32,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think things are niccā and occasionally they're aniccā, without realizing that, no, they're aniccā always, they don't change.

18
00:02:33,229 --> 00:02:38,451
[SPEAKER_01]: And your sense of niccā is only relative, like slightly less aniccā but still aniccā, so to speak.

19
00:02:39,551 --> 00:02:44,113
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that, like, uncertainty is the main characteristic of nibbāna.

20
00:02:44,393 --> 00:02:46,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But for somebody who has developed nibbāna,

21
00:02:47,302 --> 00:02:51,088
[SPEAKER_01]: That uncertainty is just the universal nature of things.

22
00:02:51,128 --> 00:02:59,721
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha did speak to lay people or somebody who didn't have the right view, and they understood... Because, you know, what is it, the nibbāna, that you teach?

23
00:02:59,781 --> 00:03:01,223
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the highest goal of your teaching?

24
00:03:01,704 --> 00:03:02,605
[SPEAKER_01]: And he would describe it.

25
00:03:03,435 --> 00:03:06,697
[SPEAKER_01]: What nibbāna is, and they would become distraught with fear.

26
00:03:07,577 --> 00:03:11,039
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you know, fall on the ground, beat their breasts, pull their hair in anguish and anxieties.

27
00:03:11,519 --> 00:03:16,462
[SPEAKER_01]: That, what this reckless teaches, will kill us, will destroy us.

28
00:03:16,522 --> 00:03:25,987
[SPEAKER_01]: So you train your mind, because if you train it, the uncertainty of nibbāna, the dispassion of nibbāna, will be the utmost pleasure, the highest bliss.

29
00:03:26,608 --> 00:03:30,870
[SPEAKER_01]: But as the Buddha said, the neutral feeling, for example, needs to be known and understood.

30
00:03:31,170 --> 00:03:31,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, this is what...

31
00:03:31,991 --> 00:03:36,014
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not known and not understood, it's going to be felt extremely unpleasantly.

32
00:03:36,174 --> 00:03:37,555
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be felt painfully.

33
00:03:38,035 --> 00:03:38,896
[SPEAKER_01]: The neutral feeling.

34
00:03:39,396 --> 00:03:47,523
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it requires wisdom to be able to truly recognize what neutral feeling is and find the peace in it.

35
00:03:48,103 --> 00:03:49,925
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes wisdom for peace.

36
00:03:49,945 --> 00:03:53,007
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just, oh, I'm peaceful, because the peaceful feeling arose in me.

37
00:03:53,607 --> 00:03:55,309
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not really the peace we're talking about.

38
00:03:57,384 --> 00:04:13,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically speaking, if you, even as a non-Arahant, want to contemplate the... well, want to have a theme for your contemplation, that will approximate the nibbāna uncertainty, the ultimate fundamental uncertainty.

39
00:04:13,275 --> 00:04:21,601
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why mindfulness of death, one of the quickest ways for purifying mind of lust and reaching nibbāna, also the sharpest way that can harm you.

40
00:04:22,827 --> 00:04:29,413
[SPEAKER_01]: The other one, when he said, the Buddha said that contemplation like, it will not be, it will not be for me, it might not be, and it won't be for me.

41
00:04:29,673 --> 00:04:41,144
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like, when he said that, that's like the highest contemplative view, that would result in serious amount of dispassion and abandoning of the world, that even an outsider can't practice.

42
00:04:42,044 --> 00:04:43,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Somebody who hasn't understood the dhamma.

43
00:04:45,127 --> 00:04:49,151
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point being, so what would be the main characteristic of that view, would be the uncertainty.

44
00:04:50,111 --> 00:04:52,053
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be, it might not be.

45
00:04:52,413 --> 00:04:57,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, it won't be for me, as in, it won't be taken as mine.

46
00:04:57,497 --> 00:05:01,760
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the main characteristic of mindfulness of death that scares people a lot?

47
00:05:02,721 --> 00:05:09,306
[SPEAKER_01]: It is the uncertainty, the implicit uncertainty of every enduring experience that you have here and now.

48
00:05:10,237 --> 00:05:13,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the Buddha said, how is the mindfulness of death practiced rightly?

49
00:05:14,078 --> 00:05:21,459
[SPEAKER_01]: It was when the monk described, I practice it like after an in-breath, I'm already practicing mindfulness before the next out-breath.

50
00:05:21,499 --> 00:05:24,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, yeah, that's as close as it needs to get.

51
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:31,901
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean, as I said before, that he's focused so hard on every moment of in-breath-death, out-breath-death.

52
00:05:31,941 --> 00:05:32,061
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

53
00:05:32,081 --> 00:05:36,062
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means that his context is so firmly established

54
00:05:36,773 --> 00:05:47,119
[SPEAKER_01]: On the... like a thorough sort of uncertainty that underlies his entire existence, that he doesn't lapse even for a moment of in or out breath.

55
00:05:47,219 --> 00:05:53,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean he goes moment in breath, moment death, moment out breath, moment death, moment in breath, moment death.

56
00:05:53,863 --> 00:05:54,023
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

57
00:05:54,323 --> 00:05:59,046
[SPEAKER_01]: Means the context is so strong that even a little lapse does not occur.

58
00:06:00,203 --> 00:06:01,184
[SPEAKER_01]: And why it is so strong?

59
00:06:01,264 --> 00:06:02,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is true.

60
00:06:03,246 --> 00:06:06,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is factually uncertain on that level.

61
00:06:06,710 --> 00:06:08,512
[SPEAKER_01]: For in-breath, you might not have an out-breath.

62
00:06:10,654 --> 00:06:14,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about, like, boiling down to a moment to catch it.

63
00:06:14,258 --> 00:06:18,443
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like just sustaining the context and recognizing the blatant truth of your existence.

64
00:06:19,581 --> 00:06:23,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about fabricating that, as an idea.

65
00:06:23,364 --> 00:06:24,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

66
00:06:24,365 --> 00:06:25,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about fabricating.

67
00:06:25,446 --> 00:06:30,029
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I said, it needs to start, like, only bhāna is the highest bliss.

68
00:06:30,449 --> 00:06:32,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, eventually.

69
00:06:33,952 --> 00:06:35,333
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you arrive at the highest bliss?

70
00:06:35,853 --> 00:06:37,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Through a magical method and technique?

71
00:06:38,275 --> 00:06:41,538
[SPEAKER_00]: By letting the truth take root, you know, take over.

72
00:06:41,558 --> 00:06:48,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by recognizing, basically, again, as the whole dhamma is, the universal characteristics of your existence.

73
00:06:48,925 --> 00:06:53,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Which are anicca, uncertainty, dukkha, unsatisfactoriness.

74
00:06:54,047 --> 00:07:01,730
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole experience is an experience of not-self, because you cannot own that which is uncertain, because sense of ownership requires sense of certainty.

75
00:07:02,770 --> 00:07:04,171
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the form of determination.

76
00:07:04,231 --> 00:07:11,414
[SPEAKER_01]: So, uncertain means undetermined, or less determined, depending on how far you push the uncertainty.

77
00:07:11,454 --> 00:07:16,596
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to contemplate a phenomenon that approximates nibbāna, even as a puthujjana, contemplate uncertainty.

78
00:07:17,687 --> 00:07:20,789
[SPEAKER_01]: But, contemplate that uncertainty, as in, start feeling it.

79
00:07:21,589 --> 00:07:26,952
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate yourself within, basically, fully enclosed in a cage of uncertainty.

80
00:07:27,372 --> 00:07:33,255
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no outside of that uncertainty, and everything that's inside, you don't really know what's subjected to from the outside.

81
00:07:33,335 --> 00:07:35,616
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are fully uncertain, top to bottom.

82
00:07:36,357 --> 00:07:42,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, what people would tend to do, same with the practice of anicca, is like, oh, then you just start determining

83
00:07:43,644 --> 00:07:46,606
[SPEAKER_01]: the uncertainty over there, or determining anicca over there.

84
00:07:46,866 --> 00:07:51,029
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you start determining what's the undetermined, which means it's not really undetermined.

85
00:07:51,569 --> 00:07:53,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when people go around, yes, yes, everything is anicca.

86
00:07:53,671 --> 00:07:56,593
[SPEAKER_01]: See, this is anicca, this is anicca, it's all anicca.

87
00:07:57,630 --> 00:08:00,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, true, actually, theoretically.

88
00:08:00,972 --> 00:08:03,234
[SPEAKER_01]: But practically, that's not the anicca that matters.

89
00:08:03,874 --> 00:08:06,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca that matters is on the level of your existence.

90
00:08:06,656 --> 00:08:10,538
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't be able to just say, oh, yeah, look, my eyes are anicca.

91
00:08:11,118 --> 00:08:11,799
[SPEAKER_01]: I can see that.

92
00:08:12,399 --> 00:08:13,900
[SPEAKER_01]: My ears are anicca, and so on.

93
00:08:14,640 --> 00:08:15,801
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because you would feel that.

94
00:08:15,941 --> 00:08:25,107
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start feeling your body being under a threat of its existence, even if you say or not say, these eyes are anicca, these ears are anicca, you're gonna feel it.

95
00:08:25,808 --> 00:08:29,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is factually anicca, and your life depends on it, yet it's uncertain.

96
00:08:31,652 --> 00:08:35,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you contemplate uncertainty, you will have to feel it.

97
00:08:35,915 --> 00:08:46,962
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the sīla needs to have been established beforehand, not acting out of greed, aversion, delusion, because, yeah, some minds will not be able to bear the recognition of what nibbāna is, as they didn't when the Buddha told them.

98
00:08:48,843 --> 00:08:54,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if the mind is strong enough, it will not get distraught in the face of anxiety towards uncertainty.

99
00:08:55,157 --> 00:08:58,438
[SPEAKER_01]: He would be like, wow, I better do something about it.

100
00:08:58,458 --> 00:09:02,279
[SPEAKER_01]: Like that king, when the Buddha told him, like, there are four mountains coming from every direction, about to crush you.

101
00:09:02,299 --> 00:09:02,939
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you gonna do?

102
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:09,961
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, first is this mountain from the east, is all around west, there's mountain from west, all around north, there's mountain from the north, all around south, mountain from the south.

103
00:09:10,442 --> 00:09:16,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, in that case, I will quickly renounce this life and, you know, he's like, well, there are four mountains coming, so do what you see fit.

104
00:09:16,683 --> 00:09:20,585
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't lose his mind, obviously, but some people got

105
00:09:23,267 --> 00:09:27,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody can feel that uncertainty.

106
00:09:28,931 --> 00:09:34,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's... for a patujana, for someone who's not trained, it will be anxiety.

107
00:09:35,114 --> 00:09:35,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

108
00:09:35,695 --> 00:09:39,557
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that anxiety can proliferate into all sorts of phobias or whatever.

109
00:09:39,637 --> 00:09:39,957
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

110
00:09:40,258 --> 00:09:41,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Fears and all kinds, you know.

111
00:09:43,379 --> 00:09:49,043
[SPEAKER_00]: But once, if you can handle and see that anxiety or that uncertainty...

112
00:09:49,143 --> 00:09:50,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainty, yeah.

113
00:09:50,724 --> 00:09:52,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Endure that uncertainty,

114
00:09:52,765 --> 00:09:58,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you are able, and again, you will be able, if you actually made the effort to practice sensory straightening beforehand.

115
00:09:59,350 --> 00:10:04,954
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason people become mad is because people perform mad actions.

116
00:10:05,094 --> 00:10:06,075
[SPEAKER_01]: They do mad stuff.

117
00:10:06,175 --> 00:10:07,256
[SPEAKER_01]: They say mad stuff.

118
00:10:07,916 --> 00:10:10,218
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot be mad if you don't act out madly.

119
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:13,861
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not mad then.

120
00:10:13,881 --> 00:10:15,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter what goes through your head.

121
00:10:15,082 --> 00:10:18,244
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't act out on your thoughts, you're not mad.

122
00:10:18,625 --> 00:10:21,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Madness becomes when you act out every single time.

123
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have practiced restraint, you will be able to not act out.

124
00:10:25,505 --> 00:10:28,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why this is necessary is because anxiety is not uncertainty.

125
00:10:28,751 --> 00:10:29,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncertainty is not anxiety.

126
00:10:30,654 --> 00:10:33,859
[SPEAKER_01]: But for an untrained mind, uncertainty is anxious.

127
00:10:35,023 --> 00:10:44,808
[SPEAKER_01]: But for a trained mind, they got used to uncertainty, and recognize it, and started understanding it, practicing its way towards nibbāna, and complete cooling down on account of uncertainty.

128
00:10:45,048 --> 00:10:46,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, by the way, that's how dispassion happens.

129
00:10:47,510 --> 00:10:55,634
[SPEAKER_01]: When the mind realizes this is not worth clinging to, this is not worth owning, or acting out, or possessing, or indulging in.

130
00:10:56,034 --> 00:11:00,417
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unworthy of that effort, because it's so thoroughly uncertain.

131
00:11:01,686 --> 00:11:05,069
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind that does start practicing like that towards uncertainty, you get used to it.

132
00:11:05,289 --> 00:11:07,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, you will not feel anxiety towards it.

133
00:11:07,912 --> 00:11:20,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the anxiety aspect of uncertainty was due to your ignorance in regard to uncertainty, not because of uncertainty.

134
00:11:20,623 --> 00:11:27,490
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't be becoming dispassionate if you don't actually sit with that or

135
00:11:28,709 --> 00:11:34,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, dispassion is a result of you basically seeing things rightly.

136
00:11:34,314 --> 00:11:36,296
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something you can just choose to do.

137
00:11:36,416 --> 00:11:37,657
[SPEAKER_01]: I choose to be dispassionate.

138
00:11:37,697 --> 00:11:38,938
[SPEAKER_01]: I choose to do anatta.

139
00:11:39,199 --> 00:11:41,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I choose to be anatta or something like that.

140
00:11:41,821 --> 00:11:47,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Dispassion, relinquishing of ownership, will be the result of recognizing that that

141
00:11:48,689 --> 00:11:51,430
[SPEAKER_01]: which you are passionate towards, and owning, and so on.

142
00:11:51,650 --> 00:11:56,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Your existence, your sense bases, your whole body, your whole life, is not worth clinging to.

143
00:11:57,151 --> 00:11:58,211
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not worth owning.

144
00:11:58,511 --> 00:12:01,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but once you don't... then, oh, it's not worth owning.

145
00:12:01,572 --> 00:12:03,012
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll... let me not own it.

146
00:12:03,252 --> 00:12:03,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

147
00:12:03,912 --> 00:12:04,713
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be automated.

148
00:12:04,733 --> 00:12:05,573
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be... that's what I mean.

149
00:12:05,593 --> 00:12:06,233
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be a result.

150
00:12:06,353 --> 00:12:09,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Like... like you have a headache due to some bacterial infection.

151
00:12:09,974 --> 00:12:10,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You take antibiotics.

152
00:12:11,094 --> 00:12:12,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You get rid of the bacteria.

153
00:12:12,274 --> 00:12:13,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Headache disappears.

154
00:12:13,375 --> 00:12:15,035
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to go and take a headache pill now.

155
00:12:15,595 --> 00:12:19,636
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the headache was the symptom of the infection, the high fever or whatever it was.

156
00:12:21,616 --> 00:12:24,717
[SPEAKER_00]: So, going back a bit, you're talking about neutral feeling.

157
00:12:25,237 --> 00:12:25,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Uh-huh.

158
00:12:25,937 --> 00:12:29,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling which is neither pleasant or unpleasant.

159
00:12:29,657 --> 00:12:32,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... in a way, you can use that word undetermined.

160
00:12:32,758 --> 00:12:33,618
[SPEAKER_00]: It's undetermined.

161
00:12:33,638 --> 00:12:35,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, already it's less determined.

162
00:12:35,458 --> 00:12:36,119
[SPEAKER_01]: It's less determined.

163
00:12:36,139 --> 00:12:37,999
[SPEAKER_01]: It's certainly not determined as agreeable.

164
00:12:38,559 --> 00:12:44,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's... you can say it's determined as neither determined as agreeable nor determined as an unagreeable, disagreeable.

165
00:12:45,822 --> 00:12:48,584
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is this important?

166
00:12:49,724 --> 00:12:52,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there are only three types of feelings.

167
00:12:52,386 --> 00:12:59,130
[SPEAKER_01]: In nibbāna, for an arahant, whoever has purified his experience, anāgāmī, it's not like a new feeling that he has on top of these three.

168
00:13:00,491 --> 00:13:02,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the existence is the five aggregates.

169
00:13:02,672 --> 00:13:09,776
[SPEAKER_01]: For puthujjana, for an arahant, the difference is the level of passion and delight in the five aggregates or freedom from those things, from the five aggregates.

170
00:13:10,965 --> 00:13:26,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's important because you start, as a puthujjana, always start basically on the level of full infatuation, full ownership, full passion, full dependence on pleasure, full avoidance of pain, full ignorance of neither pleasure nor pain.

171
00:13:27,803 --> 00:13:29,265
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see that, just like...

172
00:13:30,189 --> 00:13:32,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom is always like the practical example.

173
00:13:32,812 --> 00:13:36,695
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's neither pleasant or unpleasant, but it's really unpleasant, because there is nothing happening.

174
00:13:36,715 --> 00:13:40,499
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing felt more determinately, so to speak.

175
00:13:42,012 --> 00:13:55,222
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, like even you can see that even with people who become heavily dependent on sensuality, just slight reduction in the level of feeling that they have on account of sensuality, will feel like, oh, pain.

176
00:13:56,043 --> 00:13:59,065
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the pleasure, but not enough pleasure, it becomes extreme pain.

177
00:13:59,545 --> 00:14:05,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you become so much more dependent now on even more determined feeling, and more determined feeling, and more determined feeling.

178
00:14:07,425 --> 00:14:15,110
[SPEAKER_01]: And that determination comes directly from ignorance, from ignoring the certain truth, the certain nature of what's right in front of you.

179
00:14:17,612 --> 00:14:19,213
[SPEAKER_01]: So, avijjā, pācchā, sankhāra.

180
00:14:20,354 --> 00:14:23,156
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a metaphysical relationship between the two.

181
00:14:24,177 --> 00:14:33,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing the nature of the feeling, not understanding that it's not worth delighting to, you're automatically determining it as more and more delightful.

182
00:14:34,005 --> 00:14:37,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Escape from pain becomes more delightful than the actual pain.

183
00:14:38,229 --> 00:14:48,419
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, people who become so soft mentally, so touchy, so sort of weak, they would then find pain.

184
00:14:49,232 --> 00:14:52,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Just in a in a lack of pleasure and they will try to have to get rid of it.

185
00:14:52,735 --> 00:15:01,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's like the people is like everything is comfortable, but then these tiny little things and they can't let go of the minds, cannot let go of the tiny little imperfection, so to speak.

186
00:15:01,783 --> 00:15:10,792
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, that experience starts to revolve around the pain, on account of getting rid of that one imperfection, ignoring the reality of their experience as a whole, which is far more pleasant already.

187
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:13,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what they've been cultivating.

188
00:15:13,641 --> 00:15:15,563
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what they've been determining repeatedly.

189
00:15:16,303 --> 00:15:17,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking for faults, looking for pain.

190
00:15:18,204 --> 00:15:20,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Fearing the pain, then, obviously, because you start seeing it more and more.

191
00:15:21,767 --> 00:15:31,454
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a dog who tries to outrun the post that it's tied to, and ends up being completely, like, revolving around it until the chain is so tight, now it cannot move.

192
00:15:31,494 --> 00:15:37,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, all you see is just pain here, and pain there, and trouble here, and anxiety there, and over there.

193
00:15:38,352 --> 00:15:40,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But first thing you have to do is not act out of it.

194
00:15:40,533 --> 00:15:41,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Start practicing sensory strength.

195
00:15:42,993 --> 00:15:45,054
[SPEAKER_01]: That will enable you to... You still have to endure it.

196
00:15:45,634 --> 00:15:47,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Endurance of feelings is not optional.

197
00:15:48,815 --> 00:15:49,895
[SPEAKER_01]: The only difference...

198
00:15:50,315 --> 00:15:53,456
[SPEAKER_01]: The only thing that is optional is how you're gonna go about it.

199
00:15:54,117 --> 00:16:00,358
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you're trying to get rid of the feeling, your whole life is about finding pleasure, avoiding pain and distracting yourself.

200
00:16:01,039 --> 00:16:04,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You do that fully on the basis of having to endure these things when they're felt.

201
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:05,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't feel them.

202
00:16:07,670 --> 00:16:12,072
[SPEAKER_01]: If you choose to practice sense-restraint and endure it on those terms, you still have to endure it.

203
00:16:12,712 --> 00:16:17,494
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a benefit of that, because you can actually... sense-restraint first can result in wisdom.

204
00:16:18,615 --> 00:16:19,455
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha said.

205
00:16:19,755 --> 00:16:27,179
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace the pains of a householder life, householder mind, with the pains of a renunciate life, renunciate mind.

206
00:16:27,359 --> 00:16:27,999
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace them.

207
00:16:28,479 --> 00:16:29,040
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not optional.

208
00:16:29,200 --> 00:16:29,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is not optional.

209
00:16:31,207 --> 00:16:32,188
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling is not optional.

210
00:16:32,668 --> 00:16:38,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you have... you replace the householder's joys and happiness with the happiness of renunciation.

211
00:16:41,413 --> 00:16:43,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, feeling will have to be there.

212
00:16:44,135 --> 00:16:45,055
[SPEAKER_01]: You are subjected to it.

213
00:16:45,115 --> 00:16:46,156
[SPEAKER_01]: You will have to endure it.

214
00:16:49,038 --> 00:16:50,679
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, it's just how you go about it.

215
00:16:51,299 --> 00:16:57,743
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the neutral feeling is that recognition that comes out of wisdom, that the neutral feeling is by far the best.

216
00:16:58,609 --> 00:17:01,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Because pleasure pressures you towards more pleasure.

217
00:17:01,852 --> 00:17:06,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain, obviously, pressures you towards getting rid of it, dealing with it, quickly addressing it, manage it, get rid of it.

218
00:17:08,059 --> 00:17:10,982
[SPEAKER_00]: Neutral feeling, you realize, oh, it's undetermined.

219
00:17:11,883 --> 00:17:13,465
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm undetermined in a neutral feeling.

220
00:17:13,565 --> 00:17:14,265
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not pressured.

221
00:17:15,214 --> 00:17:20,117
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, it's your ignorance of the neutral feeling that then pressures you towards distraction and delusion, as the Buddha would say.

222
00:17:20,737 --> 00:17:25,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when not known neutral feeling, you're going to be unpleasant about it.

223
00:17:25,519 --> 00:17:28,581
[SPEAKER_01]: When known, oh, you're going to be very pleasant about it.

224
00:17:29,441 --> 00:17:33,023
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to know... knowing neutral feeling...

225
00:17:33,123 --> 00:17:40,207
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to know the neutral feeling, you have to start restraining the delight and pleasure, and you have to stop trying to get rid of the pain.

226
00:17:41,002 --> 00:17:46,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Because neutral feeling is a result of restraint.

227
00:17:47,266 --> 00:17:48,887
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kind of... Well, no.

228
00:17:48,907 --> 00:17:50,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral feeling is on the level of three feelings.

229
00:17:51,708 --> 00:17:59,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But you understanding the neutral feeling will be the result of restraint and seeing through the nature of the other two feelings that are more obvious.

230
00:18:00,933 --> 00:18:02,614
[SPEAKER_01]: But neutral feeling was there anyway.

231
00:18:03,335 --> 00:18:10,519
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize many times in the past, even as a complete kutujana, you had neutral feelings, but you never understood the value of it.

232
00:18:14,297 --> 00:18:25,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for that, you need the sensory strength in regard to positive feeling and the negative feeling developed beforehand, thoroughly, and then start seeing the nature of it through that endurance.

233
00:18:26,564 --> 00:18:28,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you see the value in neutral?

234
00:18:29,285 --> 00:18:29,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

235
00:18:29,505 --> 00:18:31,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the value in uncertainty.

236
00:18:31,946 --> 00:18:35,048
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the value in neither positive nor negative.

237
00:18:35,568 --> 00:18:37,289
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the value in non-disturbance.

238
00:18:39,510 --> 00:18:40,871
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the value in dispassion.

239
00:18:41,792 --> 00:18:43,953
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the value in disenchantment.

240
00:18:45,222 --> 00:18:47,504
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, most people don't see the value in this passion.

241
00:18:49,286 --> 00:18:53,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, passion is good, provides me with pleasure, ambition, drive for life.

242
00:18:54,631 --> 00:18:57,574
[SPEAKER_01]: And, from that point of view, yeah, that's the householder joy.

243
00:18:58,295 --> 00:19:06,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in order to have that joy, means you also have to be blind to the implicit danger of that same very thing that you're passionate about.

244
00:19:07,768 --> 00:19:15,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you do become aware of the danger, as the Buddha would say, you discern that the danger that's apparent, then you realize, oh, it's not worth clinging to it.

245
00:19:17,210 --> 00:19:18,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, it's not a choice, really.

246
00:19:18,550 --> 00:19:21,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I choose then to stop clinging to this or something.

247
00:19:22,171 --> 00:19:22,451
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

248
00:19:22,551 --> 00:19:24,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Automatically, you realize, oh, it's a trap.

249
00:19:24,772 --> 00:19:26,372
[SPEAKER_01]: And no trap is worth being trapped by.

250
00:19:27,292 --> 00:19:29,973
[SPEAKER_01]: So, only time you get trapped by a trap is when you don't see it as a trap.

251
00:19:33,113 --> 00:19:35,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contemplate uncertainty as being

252
00:19:36,926 --> 00:19:37,867
[SPEAKER_01]: It's you being within it.

253
00:19:38,888 --> 00:19:44,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Not as an object of your attention, but as a background of your experience as a whole.

254
00:19:44,352 --> 00:19:48,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Start seeing the characteristics of uncertainty.

255
00:19:52,498 --> 00:19:54,119
[SPEAKER_01]: And you develop that.

256
00:19:54,540 --> 00:19:55,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't act out of it.

257
00:19:55,821 --> 00:19:57,442
[SPEAKER_01]: Sustain your restraint and your sīla.

258
00:19:58,363 --> 00:20:01,485
[SPEAKER_01]: You will force your mind to become dispassionate.

259
00:20:03,587 --> 00:20:04,788
[SPEAKER_01]: To become disenchanted.

260
00:20:06,046 --> 00:20:10,128
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when the mind starts seeing clearly, when the clouds of the dust of passion disappears.

261
00:20:18,632 --> 00:20:25,436
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know, they say, anicca, sabhe, saṅkhara, anicca.

262
00:20:26,116 --> 00:20:31,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all determinations are uncertain.

263
00:20:33,039 --> 00:20:34,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is that then?

264
00:20:35,993 --> 00:20:42,114
[SPEAKER_01]: How will then that make those things that are determined by those determinations be?

265
00:20:43,275 --> 00:20:50,696
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if determinations are uncertain, what is then to be said about things that are being determined by those determinations?

266
00:20:50,756 --> 00:20:51,977
[SPEAKER_00]: They're even more uncertain.

267
00:20:53,497 --> 00:20:58,138
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sabhe saṅkhara anicca, sabhe saṅkhara dukkha.

268
00:21:00,403 --> 00:21:05,427
[SPEAKER_01]: All determinations are uncertain and all determinations are unsatisfactory.

269
00:21:06,128 --> 00:21:12,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, everything that's determined, sabhe dhamma, cannot be owned.

270
00:21:12,714 --> 00:21:13,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Sabhe dhamma anatta.

271
00:21:14,896 --> 00:21:15,836
[SPEAKER_00]: Determinations?

272
00:21:15,956 --> 00:21:17,718
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you mean by determinations?

273
00:21:18,519 --> 00:21:26,566
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, practically speaking, determinations, you know, you can get very technical about it and, you know, you should certainly

274
00:21:27,870 --> 00:21:31,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Familiarize yourself with the meaning of that word, definitely.

275
00:21:32,313 --> 00:21:33,734
[SPEAKER_01]: In Pali, in itself.

276
00:21:33,814 --> 00:21:40,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Just get different ideas and broaden your understanding just of the terms, so that you can then know what to recognize in your experience.

277
00:21:40,539 --> 00:21:47,624
[SPEAKER_01]: But, practically speaking, in very plain terms, determination would be anything that is a basis for your ownership.

278
00:21:48,284 --> 00:21:49,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Practically speaking.

279
00:21:50,243 --> 00:21:55,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, say, I'm very attached to my house, my family, my car, and so on.

280
00:21:56,206 --> 00:22:04,831
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can now take that the joy that you get on account of it, is determined by having the house, by having the car, by having the family.

281
00:22:05,771 --> 00:22:08,053
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, when you look at that, you can take even further.

282
00:22:08,113 --> 00:22:09,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So, determinations are not a fixed thing.

283
00:22:09,994 --> 00:22:12,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like determining a relationship.

284
00:22:12,842 --> 00:22:21,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Based on how you feel, whether you feel ignorant, infatuated, avoiding pleasure, avoiding pain, pursuing pleasure, that's going to be on the basis of something.

285
00:22:21,164 --> 00:22:23,525
[SPEAKER_01]: That basis is what determines your experience as such.

286
00:22:23,985 --> 00:22:28,327
[SPEAKER_01]: So, understanding determination frees you from these wrong things that your mind cultivates.

287
00:22:28,347 --> 00:22:31,047
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a cessation of determination, is nibbāna.

288
00:22:32,448 --> 00:22:32,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

289
00:22:33,248 --> 00:22:38,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could then say, eh... Like, you're attached to your... whatever.

290
00:22:38,970 --> 00:22:39,770
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your family,

291
00:22:41,705 --> 00:22:59,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Presence of their bodies, presence of your body, presence of your senses, presence of their senses, literally eyes and random organs, is now the basis for this entire emotional relationship, the relationship that you have with your family and your dependence and your ownership and fear if they are threatened, joy if they are successful and so on.

292
00:23:00,831 --> 00:23:05,836
[SPEAKER_01]: But, that would be inconceivable if your body would not be there, if you would not die.

293
00:23:06,537 --> 00:23:11,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fundamental one, the fundamental determinations, obviously, it's the having the body, functioning body.

294
00:23:12,183 --> 00:23:14,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your body is the fundamental determination.

295
00:23:14,645 --> 00:23:21,552
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, mindfulness of the body, done properly, maintaining the context of the body is the basis for any experience in your life.

296
00:23:22,133 --> 00:23:33,465
[SPEAKER_01]: With already... with the recognition of the uncertainty of that determination, of that context, makes then, whatever you experience on the basis of that context of your body, unownable.

297
00:23:35,188 --> 00:23:45,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Your own children, family, everything, it's still there, it's still perceived, yet now the emotional basis for your ownership is gone, evaporated, because it's seen on the basis of uncertainty.

298
00:23:46,115 --> 00:23:46,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

299
00:23:46,476 --> 00:23:50,738
[SPEAKER_00]: All your life missions, your life... Everything depends on that.

300
00:23:50,779 --> 00:23:55,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people will have to start somewhere a bit more obvious, because actually people think

301
00:23:56,182 --> 00:23:57,703
[SPEAKER_01]: I think you can just be mindful of the body.

302
00:23:57,723 --> 00:24:03,565
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes a lot of wisdom to develop this level of clarity of the context of the body as a determination for everything.

303
00:24:04,086 --> 00:24:06,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't see a connection, basically, what I'm saying.

304
00:24:06,086 --> 00:24:15,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people would not see a connection between presently enduring body as such and their dependence on the emotional joy that they get on account of their daughter getting a degree in a university or something.

305
00:24:15,470 --> 00:24:16,131
[SPEAKER_01]: They wouldn't see that.

306
00:24:17,491 --> 00:24:21,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a fundamental determination.

307
00:24:22,454 --> 00:24:30,437
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you see that determination as being anicca... Well, when you see that your own body, that you're not in control of, is perpetually...

308
00:24:30,477 --> 00:24:32,999
[SPEAKER_01]: The main characteristic of it is the uncertainty.

309
00:24:33,979 --> 00:24:35,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it doesn't matter what comes your way.

310
00:24:37,269 --> 00:24:49,372
[SPEAKER_01]: You would need to pretend that your daughter didn't get the best degree at her university, but you would be unable to forget the container, the utmost fundamental background of that experience, which is uncertainty.

311
00:24:49,892 --> 00:24:51,972
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, it will have to come in.

312
00:24:51,992 --> 00:24:53,472
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be able to get involved.

313
00:24:54,193 --> 00:25:05,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can, again, as many times, you know, in the suttas, like anāgāmīs, westerlaymen, or even like monks, arahants, you know, you still talk, discuss,

314
00:25:06,590 --> 00:25:08,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Help, give advice on mundane matters.

315
00:25:08,731 --> 00:25:10,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you stop to recognize the significance of it.

316
00:25:11,532 --> 00:25:23,576
[SPEAKER_01]: But what a parent, who has suddenly become anāgāmī or an arahant, hears that the daughter is the best at university, what they will be unable to do, is take the ownership of that experience as a whole.

317
00:25:24,036 --> 00:25:27,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they will be unable to lose the sight of the uncertainty.

318
00:25:27,857 --> 00:25:29,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they will probably, you know, just...

319
00:25:30,195 --> 00:25:35,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Help the daughter, rejoice in mundane matters, so to speak, but fundamentally be completely unmoved.

320
00:25:35,740 --> 00:25:40,104
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when they would exclaim in the Sutta, and yet my composure remained unchanged.

321
00:25:40,704 --> 00:25:44,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it was extremely positive experience or negative, the composure of the mind remained unchanged.

322
00:25:45,928 --> 00:25:50,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, my only son was being falsely accused and killed, but my composure remained unchanged.

323
00:25:51,192 --> 00:25:53,554
[SPEAKER_00]: Isn't this dhamma wonderful?

324
00:25:53,955 --> 00:26:03,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the mind established upon the undetermined, upon the uncertain, as the fundamental, like, non-negotiable basis, the truth, the reality of things, the nibbāna,

325
00:26:04,927 --> 00:26:07,788
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it's inconceivable to resist the uncertainty.

326
00:26:08,269 --> 00:26:10,710
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just because resisting the uncertainty is uncertain.

327
00:26:11,450 --> 00:26:16,652
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to hammer that through that emotional barrier that your ownership towards things is.

328
00:26:17,953 --> 00:26:21,694
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the Buddha did describe to some people what determinations are, he actually did.

329
00:26:21,915 --> 00:26:26,217
[SPEAKER_01]: He referred to exactly this, the possessions.

330
00:26:26,257 --> 00:26:28,918
[SPEAKER_01]: The king was determined by his king's possessions.

331
00:26:30,118 --> 00:26:33,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Those were his determinations of his, you know,

332
00:26:34,811 --> 00:26:35,972
[SPEAKER_01]: My sense of self.

333
00:26:52,173 --> 00:26:53,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's exactly what you realize.

334
00:26:53,714 --> 00:26:59,657
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your sense of self needs to have these determinations that you blindly own by not understanding their uncertainty.

335
00:27:00,277 --> 00:27:08,362
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point to consider here, in this case, it's not like, oh, so you just go around throwing things away and say, I don't own this, I don't own this, I don't own this.

336
00:27:08,702 --> 00:27:12,084
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do is find things that... because you can't throw away your body like that.

337
00:27:13,146 --> 00:27:16,048
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just say, okay, I throw away my senses and I'm an arahant.

338
00:27:16,068 --> 00:27:16,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't work like that.

339
00:27:17,470 --> 00:27:24,035
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you need to do is find what you do own, and through that ownership, start discerning the uncertainty of that thing.

340
00:27:24,275 --> 00:27:26,557
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's going to be felt anxiously, at least initially.

341
00:27:27,218 --> 00:27:30,621
[SPEAKER_01]: If the mind is developed, you will not suffer much on account of anxiety.

342
00:27:31,161 --> 00:27:32,782
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still the same route you have to go.

343
00:27:33,523 --> 00:27:42,794
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when the Buddha described sometimes this is a pleasant way towards arahantship, quick and pleasant, slow and pleasant, quick and painful, slow and painful.

344
00:27:43,515 --> 00:27:44,957
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same way.

345
00:27:44,977 --> 00:27:49,122
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just how slow you're going to go and how much you're going to suffer on account of it or not.

346
00:27:49,943 --> 00:27:50,864
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the same way.

347
00:27:52,414 --> 00:28:00,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the sense of ownership, discerning the uncertainty, the mind that has developed samādhi will not be moved by that uncertainty, will not feel as much anxiety.

348
00:28:01,544 --> 00:28:06,430
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind that hasn't will feel a lot of it, or relatively speaking, depending on how much you develop.

349
00:28:06,650 --> 00:28:13,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So samādhi, as I said before, is not measured by how peaceful and blissful you had your meditation session until the bell rang.

350
00:28:14,238 --> 00:28:22,022
[SPEAKER_01]: It's measuring how much you don't move in the face of uncertainty, in the face of sensuality, in the face of pain, in the face of threat, misfortune.

351
00:28:22,502 --> 00:28:33,507
[SPEAKER_01]: How much is your mind, like, scraping for the walls to outrun the pain, or how much is it like the mountain that the Buddha described, that doesn't move, whether it's the wind, rain, hailstorm, or whatever else.

352
00:28:34,503 --> 00:28:35,404
[SPEAKER_01]: That is what samādhi is.

353
00:28:35,624 --> 00:28:47,239
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why exactly the beginning of the right samādhi, the first jhāna, starts through fully surmounting, not being moved by the five hindrances, or the way we say, by the joys and griefs in regard to the world.

354
00:28:48,898 --> 00:28:51,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop that, samādhi of jhāna.

355
00:28:51,680 --> 00:28:57,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I watch my nostril, I enter jhāna, and then I don't get the hindrances and joy of the world, which is what people hope for.

356
00:28:58,086 --> 00:28:58,406
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

357
00:28:58,546 --> 00:29:05,132
[SPEAKER_01]: You work through that, then the result is samādhi, then the result is the pleasure of that seclusion and safety and relief, and that's what jhāna is.

358
00:29:06,853 --> 00:29:13,959
[SPEAKER_01]: So samādhi is the measure of immovability in the face of any pressure, positive or negative, but usually negative.

359
00:29:19,940 --> 00:29:25,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And fundamentally, yeah, that was exactly the reaction of people when they heard nibbāna as the fundamental uncertainty.

360
00:29:25,622 --> 00:29:26,342
[SPEAKER_01]: They freaked out.

361
00:29:26,362 --> 00:29:27,602
[SPEAKER_01]: They had no samādhi.

362
00:29:28,422 --> 00:29:29,182
[SPEAKER_01]: They had no sīla.

363
00:29:29,583 --> 00:29:30,403
[SPEAKER_01]: They had no samādhi.

364
00:29:31,103 --> 00:29:34,444
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they were completely overwhelmed when they understood what the Buddha was saying.

365
00:29:35,624 --> 00:29:36,985
[SPEAKER_01]: What his teaching is.

366
00:29:37,025 --> 00:29:37,785
[SPEAKER_01]: That will kill me.

367
00:29:37,845 --> 00:29:38,665
[SPEAKER_01]: That will destroy me.

368
00:29:38,705 --> 00:29:40,185
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the true death of me.

369
00:29:41,166 --> 00:29:43,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Because from Buddhism's point of view, it is.

370
00:29:44,086 --> 00:29:45,867
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why sīla, samādhi, pañña.

371
00:29:47,185 --> 00:29:51,091
[SPEAKER_01]: You practice sīla, you practice sensory strain, you invest the effort, you grind, you endure.

372
00:29:51,532 --> 00:29:52,874
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a samādhi already.

373
00:29:53,835 --> 00:29:57,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, now I watch my nostrils to get the samādhi and peaceful experience.

374
00:29:57,921 --> 00:29:58,061
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

375
00:29:58,402 --> 00:30:01,767
[SPEAKER_01]: You already have samādhi to the extent you are able to not break your precepts.

376
00:30:02,956 --> 00:30:07,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Because to that extent, you're able to endure the negative pressure, the pull of sensuality.

377
00:30:07,518 --> 00:30:13,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is going to... and the more you make things clearer, the more pressure you're going to see that's there.

378
00:30:13,801 --> 00:30:14,701
[SPEAKER_00]: You can see that.

379
00:30:14,841 --> 00:30:16,102
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure becomes even clearer.

380
00:30:16,342 --> 00:30:17,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Becomes even clearer.

381
00:30:17,223 --> 00:30:26,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I said it the other day that I start meditation, and if I'm doing it right, I'm supposed to have pleasant feelings, bliss, joy, rapture.

382
00:30:26,927 --> 00:30:28,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

383
00:30:28,408 --> 00:30:29,868
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's actually the opposite.

384
00:30:30,209 --> 00:30:31,009
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely opposite.

385
00:30:31,612 --> 00:30:40,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi is the work that basically is born out of your work of your sīla, work of your sense restraint.

386
00:30:42,340 --> 00:30:44,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just so I keep the precepts, I don't think about them.

387
00:30:44,542 --> 00:30:47,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I also practice sense restraint in between keeping the precepts?

388
00:30:49,365 --> 00:30:54,649
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I just let my eyes see whatever they want, ears chase the sounds, body chase the touches?

389
00:30:54,709 --> 00:30:58,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do I actually impose sense restraint on that?

390
00:30:58,970 --> 00:31:01,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sīla, samādhi is the result of it.

391
00:31:01,913 --> 00:31:09,059
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that's then, you see, oh, so you develop samādhi, not by watching your nostrils, but by fortifying that which your sīla created for you.

392
00:31:10,180 --> 00:31:11,101
[SPEAKER_01]: And what's the result then?

393
00:31:11,321 --> 00:31:15,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Sīla, samādhi, ah, pañña, clear seeing, understanding through what you have cultivated.

394
00:31:17,833 --> 00:31:18,754
[SPEAKER_01]: But more often than not.

395
00:31:19,294 --> 00:31:20,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, sīla is over there.

396
00:31:20,555 --> 00:31:21,656
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm a good person.

397
00:31:22,136 --> 00:31:23,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me give me the technique.

398
00:31:23,437 --> 00:31:23,797
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me watch.

399
00:31:23,817 --> 00:31:27,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, let me have special blissful experience when my body trembles with joy and so on.

400
00:31:28,580 --> 00:31:33,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the experience of that must be some sort of wisdom in jhāna, in my pañña.

401
00:31:33,524 --> 00:31:35,465
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus, that's my practice toward arahant.

402
00:31:35,645 --> 00:31:36,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely upside down.

403
00:31:39,388 --> 00:31:41,329
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just chasing another agreeable pleasure.

404
00:31:42,430 --> 00:31:45,052
[SPEAKER_01]: You damage your samādhi by ability to withstand pain.

405
00:31:46,173 --> 00:31:54,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally, you measure it basically by, you know, how deeply you were away when you were sleeping or meditating.

406
00:31:58,178 --> 00:32:01,899
[SPEAKER_01]: It's fine if you get blissful experiences, but don't measure your samādhi by that.

407
00:32:02,160 --> 00:32:02,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite the opposite.

408
00:32:04,140 --> 00:32:05,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Take them with a pinch of salt.

409
00:32:06,641 --> 00:32:13,264
[SPEAKER_01]: And as samādhi is when my mind is throughout a day not moved by joys and griefs in regard to the world.

410
00:32:13,304 --> 00:32:13,924
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the world?

411
00:32:14,044 --> 00:32:14,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally everything.

412
00:32:16,160 --> 00:32:18,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touch is the world for your mind.

413
00:32:19,703 --> 00:32:21,105
[SPEAKER_00]: And your mind is not moved by anything.

414
00:32:21,165 --> 00:32:22,186
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what samādhi is.

415
00:32:22,626 --> 00:32:30,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, I had such a powerful samādhi, I was unaware of the world for three hours, which is the same when I go to sleep.

416
00:32:30,534 --> 00:32:31,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's my samādhi.

417
00:32:31,815 --> 00:32:31,975
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

418
00:32:32,556 --> 00:32:36,480
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just probably being asleep or switched off, for whatever reason.

419
00:32:40,585 --> 00:32:49,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Abandoning the joys and griefs in regard to the world and basically cultivating the context of uncertainty will force you... will compose your mind.

420
00:32:49,793 --> 00:32:54,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Force your mind into samādhi is another way for saying samādhi.

421
00:32:55,859 --> 00:32:57,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi is composing.

422
00:32:58,942 --> 00:32:59,562
[SPEAKER_00]: Bringing it together.

423
00:32:59,582 --> 00:33:00,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Bringing it together.

424
00:33:00,603 --> 00:33:01,123
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping it together.

425
00:33:01,143 --> 00:33:02,745
[SPEAKER_01]: That which is loose and scattered.

426
00:33:03,345 --> 00:33:06,028
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's going to be some effort involved there.

427
00:33:08,103 --> 00:33:09,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Effortless driving.

428
00:33:09,304 --> 00:33:09,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

429
00:33:09,884 --> 00:33:12,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Effortless driving through rounding up the precepts, the sensory strain.

430
00:33:13,745 --> 00:33:15,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Tighter, tighter, tighter.

431
00:33:15,746 --> 00:33:17,787
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have composed now what you need to see.

432
00:33:22,328 --> 00:33:24,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to do meditation session?

433
00:33:25,510 --> 00:33:28,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Sit down and then see.

434
00:33:29,191 --> 00:33:32,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring up the joys and griefs of the world and see how much your mind moves.

435
00:33:32,292 --> 00:33:34,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Bring up the liability, as we often say.

436
00:33:35,182 --> 00:33:35,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm fine now.

437
00:33:36,445 --> 00:33:37,508
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not grieving over anything.

438
00:33:37,909 --> 00:33:38,350
[SPEAKER_01]: Khudai!

439
00:33:39,874 --> 00:33:42,259
[SPEAKER_01]: What if my next phone call is bad news?

440
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:44,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I be distraught?

441
00:33:44,600 --> 00:33:51,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I know for sure that, as the anāgāmīs in the suttas exclaim, isn't it wonderful, my composure remains unchanged?

442
00:33:52,123 --> 00:33:52,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Can I say that?

443
00:33:53,463 --> 00:34:03,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Do I know for sure my composure will remain unchanged regardless of the news that comes that my only son is being falsely imprisoned and just murdered, and my composure remains unchanged?

444
00:34:03,167 --> 00:34:09,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Not that because I didn't hear the news rightly, or I don't know the significance of my only son, otherwise she wouldn't have said my only son.

445
00:34:12,310 --> 00:34:16,073
[SPEAKER_01]: She didn't engage in, oh, it's just matter, it's just elements, it's just anicca.

446
00:34:16,273 --> 00:34:16,453
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

447
00:34:16,693 --> 00:34:26,841
[SPEAKER_01]: My only son, that I've bred, raised, protected, a good person, falsely imprisoned by the king, falsely accused, falsely tried and executed.

448
00:34:28,222 --> 00:34:34,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully aware of the significance of this extreme personal injustice.

449
00:34:34,686 --> 00:34:36,928
[SPEAKER_01]: And the mind remained unchanged.

450
00:34:36,968 --> 00:34:37,569
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't it?

451
00:34:37,849 --> 00:34:39,330
[SPEAKER_01]: My composure remained unchanged.

452
00:34:40,309 --> 00:34:41,770
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is that samādhi?

453
00:34:42,310 --> 00:34:42,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you know that?

454
00:34:43,870 --> 00:34:46,791
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I liable to things changing my composure?

455
00:34:47,051 --> 00:34:49,892
[SPEAKER_01]: If so, that's your work.

456
00:34:50,472 --> 00:34:58,395
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unreason hindrances, can they arise and beset my mind and overwhelm it and make me run for the walls?

457
00:34:59,496 --> 00:35:01,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Or have I developed my mind to be unmoved?

458
00:35:02,737 --> 00:35:06,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're unmoved, that's when you get to see things clearly, not while you're running and shaking.

459
00:35:07,898 --> 00:35:09,159
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you need to stop moving.

460
00:35:10,302 --> 00:35:18,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop trying to... stop overly indulging in things, moving towards them, stop trying to outrun them, moving away from them, and stop turning a blind eye and ignoring them.

